course: dictionary
lesson: 1216
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning is meant by reading the words around it. This is context again. «At a buffet, taking a little and going back for more wastes less than piling up a full plate.» Here a buffet is a meal where you serve yourself, because you take food and go back for more.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «The tent was buffeted by the wind all night.» There is no food in this sentence. Buffeted means hit and pushed about again and again, because the wind keeps knocking into the tent. Ask which meaning fits the clues, then swap it into the sentence to check.
explain: Say the word carefully too. The food word is said «BUFF-ay» or «boo-FAY», while the word for hitting or pushing is said «BUFF-it». The spelling stays the same, so the clues in the sentence tell you which meaning you need.
fact: A buffet is a meal where food is set out on a table and people serve themselves.
fact: A buffet can also be a café or carriage on a train or at a station where you can buy food.
fact: To buffet is to hit or push something again and again, like strong wind or waves.
fact: An «all-you-can-eat buffet» means you pay once and eat as much as you like.
fact: Old meaning: a buffet can be a side cabinet for dishes.
exercise: «In Newcastle, Nan turns 70 and there is a buffet with sausage rolls stacked like bricks.» What is a buffet here? | A seat on a train | A hit from a strong wind | *A meal where food is set out and people serve themselves | A cabinet for old dishes
exercise: «The little boat was buffeted by huge waves all night.» What does buffeted mean? | Carried gently to the shore | Made a loud noise | Filled with food | *Hit and pushed about again and again
exercise: «Parties and hotels often have an all-you-can-eat buffet.» What does all-you-can-eat mean? | You cannot eat at all | *You pay once and eat as much as you like | You must eat everything on the plate | You can only eat one plate of food
test: «The wind buffeted the tent all night long.» What does buffeted mean? | Very quiet and still | Served with sausage rolls | Set out neatly on a table | *Pushed and hit again and again by strong wind
test: «Lunch at the wedding was a buffet, so we served ourselves.» What is a buffet here? | A cabinet for plates | A café on a train | *A meal where food is set out and people serve themselves | A hit from a wave
exam: In Lopburi there is a yearly monkey buffet festival. Which clue shows that buffet means food here? | Thailand | Yearly | *Food is set out for the monkeys | Temples
